  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why",
    body:
        "Single strokes are the most exposed sound in drumming: every uneven note is heard. Making the weaker hand match the stronger one is the most important technical habit there is, and it starts here, at low volume and low speed.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How",
    body:
        "Alternate R L R L, both sticks from the same low-to-medium height (about 10 cm), same angle, same spot on the pad. Each stroke rebounds back to its starting height. When you switch from eighths to sixteenths, the hands do not get higher or tighter, they just move twice as often.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Tips",
    body:
        "Record yourself or listen closely: can you hear which hand is which? Then it is not even yet. In line 3 the beat of sixteenths walks through the bar, count it out loud. In line 8 the single eighth note at the start of bar 2 flips the lead: from there the left hand leads.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Song examples",
    body:
        "Wipe Out (The Surfaris): the tom part is a long string of even singles. Sing, Sing, Sing (Benny Goodman, Gene Krupa on drums): steady single strokes on the toms. Smells Like Teen Spirit (Nirvana): the intro fill is sixteenth-note singles across the drums.",
  ),
